From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: John Merchant <john@johnmerchant.dev>
Date: Fri, 30 Oct 2026 02:58:08 +1030
Subject: [PATCH 21/42] hw/arm/chumby_classic: Add the backlight, LCD, speaker,
 headphone and key lines

The stock kernel's sense1 driver handles the Chumby's board-level lines:
the LCD backlight through the PWM's duty cycle (/proc/sys/sense1/
brightness), the LCD's power on keypad column 2, the speaker
amplifier's mute on column 4 (spkmute, high to mute), the headphone
jack's switch on row 3 (hpin, low while plugged in) and the top button
on row 0. None but the button reached anything outside the SoC, and the
button only through a QOM property.

Add a chumby-io device that models these lines and wire it up:

- the backlight level and the LCD's power set the LCD controller's
  brightness, so the screen dims with the backlight and goes black
  when either is off. Until the guest first programs the PWM the
  backlight is full, so kernels without a backlight driver show their
  screen.
- headphones are plugged in and out with the "headphones" property.
- the "button" property moves to the device; and a key, F12 unless
  "button-key" names another QKeyCode or none, holds the button while
  it is down, from any display's keyboard or QMP's send-key and
  input-send-event. Other keys pass on to the guest's keyboard, if it
  has one. F12 has no use in the stock firmware, which has no
  keyboard, nor in QEMU's own displays, so the button works in a
  window out of the box.
- changes are reported with the CHUMBY_* QMP events, and
  query-chumby-state returns the lot.

The device's properties are the machine's too, so -M
chumby-classic,button-key=pause and qom-set /machine headphones true
work. Speaker mute and headphones are also GPIO outputs, for an audio
model to use.

Signed-off-by: John Merchant <john@johnmerchant.dev>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01B1fN5Fc77Z6fVVe4rWJxPT
